                                                  \begin{document}$$0.7\pm 0.4{Sv}.$$\end{document}0.7±0.4Sv. (about 4% of the total transport) during the AD 850–1850. This slow weakening trend is caused by strong, explosive volcanic eruptions during the LIA. After a rapid strengthening in the initial decade after an eruption, the AMOC experiences a slow centennial timescale weakening, driven by radiatively-induced decrease in ocean heat content tied to NADW, which generates sustained surface cooling, reduced surface evaporation, and a consequent increase in the freshwater flux into the subpolar North Atlantic deep convection region. The steady increase in freshwater flux leads to a long-term decline of AMOC. Of particular note in our mechanism is the counterintuitive centennial timescale response to the accumulated impact of short-term volcanic radiative forcing events. However, this response may depend on the mean climate state and model biases62, introducing uncertainties that warrant further investigation. Despite this, this mechanism has implications for future climate projections. The current AMOC state, for example, is partly determined by the lingering effects of past explosive volcanic forcing events, and the future projected behavior of the AMOC under anthropogenic greenhouse radiative forcing should account for the delayed impact of past radiative volcanic impact on AMOC decades to centuries ago.

